<!-- ARTICLE BODY -->
<section style="background:var(--tw-white);padding:2.5rem 0 4rem;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:1040px;">

    <!-- INTRO CALLOUT -->
    <div class="tw-fade" style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-left:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:1.75rem 2rem;margin-bottom:2.5rem;">
      <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--tw-forest);line-height:1.9;margin:0;">No couple sets out to hurt each other. Most relationship damage happens not through cruelty but through patterns of communication that both partners have learned and normalized, often long before they ever met each other. Recognizing these patterns is the first real step toward changing them.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw-fade" style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.95;color:var(--tw-text-dark);">

      <!-- STAT BLOCK -->
      <div style="background:var(--tw-forest);border-radius:16px;padding:2rem 2.5rem;margin:0 0 2.5rem;display:flex;gap:2rem;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <div style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:3rem;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;line-height:1;flex-shrink:0;">93.6%</div>
        <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.95rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.85);margin:0;line-height:1.8;">Dr. John Gottman's research team at the University of Washington was able to predict which couples would separate within six years with 93.6 percent accuracy, simply by observing how they communicated during disagreements. Four specific patterns made all the difference.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">The Research Behind the Four Patterns</h2>
      <p>Over four decades of studying couples at his research facility at the University of Washington, Dr. John Gottman and his team observed thousands of couples in conversation, tracking how they talked to each other during conflict, how quickly they escalated, how they repaired, and what their bodies were doing while they spoke. From this research emerged a clear and consistent finding: four specific communication patterns predicted relationship breakdown with remarkable accuracy.</p>
      <p>Gottman named these four patterns the Four Horsemen, borrowing from the biblical image of four riders whose arrival signals devastation. The name is deliberately dramatic, because the research shows that when these patterns become entrenched, they genuinely do signal serious damage to a relationship. But the research also shows something equally important: these patterns are not permanent. They can be recognized, understood, and replaced with something better.</p>
      <p>What makes Gottman's work so useful clinically is that the four patterns tend to follow a predictable sequence. Criticism opens the door. Contempt follows when criticism becomes habitual. Contempt triggers defensiveness. And when defensiveness fails to resolve anything, stonewalling takes over. Understanding where you are in this sequence is the beginning of finding your way out of it.</p>

      <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
      <div style="margin:3rem 0;padding:2rem;border-top:2px solid var(--tw-gold);border-bottom:2px solid var(--tw-gold);text-align:center;">
        <p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.65rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--tw-forest);line-height:1.4;margin:0;">"These patterns are not evidence that you are bad at relationships. They are your nervous system doing what it was designed to do when it perceives a threat to the most important bond in your life."</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">The Four Patterns in Detail</h2>

      <!-- PATTERN 1 -->
      <div style="border:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);border-top:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;padding:2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <div style="width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;">1</div>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.5rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;">Criticism</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Criticism is the pattern most couples encounter first, and it is important to distinguish it from what Gottman calls a complaint. A complaint addresses a specific behaviour in a specific situation: "I was upset that you did not call when you were going to be late." A criticism attacks the person's character: "You never think about anyone but yourself." The shift from specific to global, from the behaviour to the person, is what makes criticism so damaging.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Criticism often carries the words "you always" or "you never." It implies that the problem is not what you did but who you are. Over time, a partner on the receiving end of consistent criticism begins to feel that they are fundamentally flawed, that nothing they do will be enough, and that the relationship is a place where they are judged rather than accepted.</p>
        <div style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:10px;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-top:1rem;">
          <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Antidote</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.92rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.75;">Use what Gottman calls a gentle start-up. Instead of "You never listen to me," try "I feel unheard when I am talking about something important and I notice your attention is elsewhere. Can we talk about this?" Describe how you feel. Name the specific situation. Express a positive need rather than attacking the person.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PATTERN 2 -->
      <div style="border:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);border-top:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;padding:2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <div style="width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;">2</div>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.5rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;">Contempt</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Contempt is the most destructive of the four patterns and the strongest predictor of relationship breakdown. While criticism says "you did something wrong," contempt says "I am better than you." It communicates superiority, disgust, and disrespect. Its expressions include eye-rolling, sarcasm, mockery, name-calling, and belittling. It is the difference between disagreeing with someone and looking down on them.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Contempt tends to develop when criticism has gone unaddressed for a long time and resentment has accumulated. The partner who was criticized feels increasingly unseen and unvalued, and eventually that unresolved hurt hardens into something colder: a genuine sense of superiority or disdain. Gottman's research found that the presence of contempt alone was enough to predict divorce with significant accuracy, even without the other three patterns.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Contempt also has measurable physical effects. Research from Gottman's lab found that partners who experienced contempt from their significant other had significantly more infectious illnesses than those who did not. The chronic stress of feeling looked down on by the person you love has real physiological consequences.</p>
        <div style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:10px;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-top:1rem;">
          <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Antidote</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.92rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.75;">Rebuilding a culture of appreciation and respect. This is not a quick fix. It requires genuinely looking for what you admire, respect, and are grateful for in your partner, and expressing it regularly. Gottman's research suggests that couples need at least five positive interactions for every negative one during conflict for the relationship to remain stable. Contempt develops when that ratio has been reversed for too long.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PATTERN 3 -->
      <div style="border:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);border-top:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;padding:2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <div style="width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;">3</div>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.5rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;">Defensiveness</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Defensiveness is the pattern that is perhaps easiest to understand in the moment, because it feels entirely justified. When you feel attacked or criticized, the instinct is to defend yourself. The problem is that defensiveness, as natural as it feels, actually sends a very clear message to your partner: your concern does not matter. I am the real victim here.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Defensiveness typically looks like making excuses, meeting a complaint with a counter-complaint, or denying responsibility entirely. "Yes, but..." is one of its most common opening moves. It shifts the conversation away from any possibility of understanding or resolution and transforms it into a competition about who is more wronged.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">What makes defensiveness particularly persistent is that it is so deeply connected to self-protection. When we feel emotionally unsafe, the instinct to defend ourselves is strong and fast. It takes real work to interrupt that reflex and replace it with something that actually helps.</p>
        <div style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:10px;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-top:1rem;">
          <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Antidote</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.92rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.75;">Taking genuine responsibility, even partial responsibility, for your role in the dynamic. It does not require you to agree with everything your partner says or to abandon your own perspective. It simply requires you to ask: is there any part of this that I can honestly acknowledge? Even "I can see why that felt frustrating" is a step toward connection rather than escalation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PATTERN 4 -->
      <div style="border:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);border-top:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;padding:2rem;margin:1.5rem 0 2.5rem;">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <div style="width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;">4</div>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.5rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;">Stonewalling</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Stonewalling is withdrawal. It is what happens when a person shuts down emotionally, stops responding, looks away, gives one-word answers, or leaves the conversation entirely, not physically necessarily, but psychologically. The stonewall goes up and the interaction hits it and stops.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Stonewalling looks, from the outside, like the stonewaller does not care. In reality, the opposite is often true. Research from Gottman's lab found that people who stonewall typically have heart rates that spike significantly during conflict. The shutdown is not indifference. It is overwhelm. The nervous system becomes so flooded that continuing to engage feels physiologically impossible.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">The difficulty is that from the other partner's perspective, stonewalling is deeply painful. It reads as abandonment, as contempt, as confirmation that their feelings do not matter. This can push the other partner to escalate, which floods the stonewall er further, which causes them to shut down more completely. It is a cycle that is very difficult to break without understanding what is actually driving it.</p>
        <div style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:10px;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-top:1rem;">
          <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Antidote</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.92rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.75;">Physiological self-soothing. When you notice you are becoming flooded, the most useful thing you can do is agree to take a break, not to avoid the conversation but to allow your nervous system to regulate before you return to it. This requires communicating the need for the break clearly rather than simply going silent: "I need twenty minutes and then I want to come back to this." The break only works if it genuinely involves calming activities rather than rehearsing grievances.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">Why These Patterns Are So Hard to Change Alone</h2>
      <p>Reading about the four patterns and understanding them intellectually is very different from being able to interrupt them in the heat of a real argument, when your heart is beating fast and you feel unheard and the person you love has just said something that stung. In those moments, the nervous system moves faster than intention.</p>
      <p>This is why couples therapy is not about being taught communication rules. It is about creating the conditions in which both partners can begin to feel safe enough to be genuinely vulnerable with each other, and in which new patterns can be practiced enough times that they start to become more instinctive than the old ones.</p>

      <!-- KEY INSIGHT BOX -->
      <div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(44,57,48,0.05) 0%,rgba(162,123,92,0.08) 100%);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border-radius:16px;padding:1.75rem 2rem;margin:2.5rem 0;">
        <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Key Insight</p>
        <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.85;">Gottman's research found that couples waited an average of six years after problems began before seeking therapy. That is six years during which the four patterns had time to become deeply entrenched and the emotional distance had time to widen significantly. The earlier couples address these patterns, the faster and more completely they can change them.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Emotionally Focused Couples Therapy, or EFCT, is the approach Shimul uses with couples at Twish. Rather than focusing primarily on communication skills, EFCT works at the level of attachment, helping partners understand the underlying emotional needs that drive their patterns. When a partner criticizes, they are usually expressing a need for closeness or reassurance in a way that backfires. When a partner stonewalls, they are usually protecting themselves from an overwhelming fear of rejection or inadequacy. EFCT helps both partners see past the surface of each other's behaviour to the attachment needs underneath, and to respond to those needs more directly.</p>
      <p>For more on what couples therapy actually involves and what to expect, you may find our article on <a href="/blog/when-to-start-therapy" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">when to start therapy</a> helpful.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">These Patterns Do Not Mean Your Relationship Is Over</h2>
      <p>One of the most important things to take from Gottman's research is that the presence of these four patterns does not mean a relationship is beyond repair. They are incredibly common. Most couples have used all four of them at some point. What the research shows is that it is the frequency, the intensity, and the absence of repair attempts that ultimately determines the outcome.</p>
      <p>Repair is one of the most important concepts in Gottman's work. A repair attempt is anything one partner does to de-escalate tension during a conflict, whether that is an apology, a moment of humour, an acknowledgment, or simply a hand on a shoulder. Couples who make frequent repair attempts, and whose partners receive them, are far more resilient than couples who do not, even when the four patterns are also present.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.gottman.com/about/research/couples/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">Gottman Institute</a> emphasizes that the goal of couples therapy is not the elimination of all conflict, which is neither possible nor desirable. The goal is to help couples navigate conflict in ways that do not damage the relationship, and to build enough of a foundation of trust, friendship, and positive connection that the relationship can weather the inevitable difficulties of two lives intertwined.</p>
      <p>If you recognize these patterns in your relationship, that recognition is not a reason to despair. It is a reason to act, because the research is clear: couples who address these patterns with the right support make real and lasting change.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">Things You Can Start Doing This Week</h2>

      <!-- TIPS BOX -->
      <div style="background:var(--tw-forest);border-radius:16px;padding:2.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0 3rem;">
        <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Before Your Next Difficult Conversation</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.25rem;">
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">1</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Check your heart rate before engaging</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">If you are already flooded before the conversation begins, you are more likely to fall into one of the four patterns. Take five minutes to calm your nervous system before raising a difficult topic.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">2</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Start with "I feel" not "You always"</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Describe your own emotional experience rather than making statements about your partner's character. This is not just politeness. It is the single most effective way to prevent defensiveness from the start.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">3</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Agree on a signal for a time-out</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Before conflict arises, decide together on a word, phrase, or gesture that means "I am getting flooded and I need twenty minutes." This makes it easier to use in the moment without it feeling like abandonment.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">4</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Name something you appreciate every day</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Contempt grows in the absence of expressed appreciation. It costs very little to say "I noticed you did that and I appreciated it." Over time, this rebuilds the emotional bank account that conflict inevitably draws from.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>If you and your partner are struggling with communication patterns that feel stuck, or if conflict in your relationship has reached a point where it feels like you are speaking different languages entirely, couples therapy can help. Shimul offers emotionally focused couples therapy in Mississauga, Markham, and virtually across Canada. The first conversation is free. You may also find it helpful to read about <a href="/blog/understanding-anxiety" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">how anxiety affects our nervous system responses</a>, since the physiology of anxiety and the physiology of relationship conflict overlap significantly.</p>

